**Builder** separates the construction of a complex object from its representation. It is useful when a class has many optional fields, so constructors with long parameter lists (telescoping constructors) are error-prone, and it lets the final object be immutable.

    public final class User {
        private final String name;      // required
        private final int age;          // optional
        private final String email;     // optional

        private User(Builder b) { name = b.name; age = b.age; email = b.email; }

        public static class Builder {
            private final String name;
            private int age;
            private String email;
            public Builder(String name) { this.name = name; }
            public Builder age(int age) { this.age = age; return this; }
            public Builder email(String e) { this.email = e; return this; }
            public User build() { return new User(this); }
        }
    }
    // User u = new User.Builder("Asha").age(21).email("a@x.com").build();

Validate required fields inside `build()` so invalid objects can never exist.

**Singleton** guarantees one instance and a global access point. Show the thread-safe versions:

    // Initialization-on-demand holder: lazy, thread-safe, no locking
    public class Config {
        private Config() {}
        private static class Holder { static final Config INSTANCE = new Config(); }
        public static Config getInstance() { return Holder.INSTANCE; }
    }

    // Enum singleton: safe against reflection and serialization
    public enum Registry { INSTANCE; }

The classic double-checked locking version also works, but the field must be `volatile`; without it another thread can see a partially constructed object.

**Mistakes to avoid**
- A lazy `getInstance()` without synchronization is not thread-safe.
- A public constructor, reflection and deserialization can all break a naive Singleton; the enum form avoids this.
- Singletons are global state that makes testing hard. Say you would usually prefer dependency injection with a single-scope bean.
